; Regression: the worst face of the dropped annotation — a TYPO IN AN EXPRESSION
; that became a binding of an unknown type (2026-08-15).
;
; An untyped binding PEELS its first operand as a type annotation
; (plan_binding#'s has_peeled_type), which is what makes `var buffer u8[64]`
; work. So `let r nosuchpeel + 1` bound `r` with type `nosuchpeel` and an
; initializer of `+ 1`, and the unknown-identifier check that guards both sides
; of a binary operator never saw the name at all: it had already been consumed as
; a type.
;
; The neighbouring shapes were all loud, which is exactly why this one was hard
; to place — `let r 1 + nosuchpeel`, `let r nosuchpeel` and
; `let r: int nosuchpeel + 1` each reported "unknown identifier". Only the
; peelable position was silent, and it is the one a typo lands in most often.
;
; xfail: unknown type: nosuchpeel

let r nosuchpeel + 1
print "unreachable"
